# Copy to .env and fill in. .env is gitignored.
#
# Every field on `core.config.Settings` can be set here, prefixed `ASSISTANT_`
# (`Settings.model_config` sets `env_file=".env"`). This file lists only the
# settings that **arm** something — leave one unset and that feature is off,
# unless its own comment says otherwise — plus the few that say what this
# deployment *is*: where its data lives, which model route it uses, what local
# time means. Every other setting — every bound, cap, timeout, retention and
# sweep cadence — has a working default and carries its own description on its
# `Settings` field in `src/ai_assistant/core/config.py`. This file deliberately
# does not copy those descriptions; read them where they live. `docs/guide/` is
# the prose walk through a first run, and names the handful of these you
# actually need.
#
# Values below are examples of the *form*, not a record of the defaults. A
# setting left commented out keeps whatever `Settings` ships.
#
# Durations take an ISO-8601 duration or a full `HH:MM:SS` clock string; a bare
# number of seconds is refused at load. `readers/calendar.py`'s module docstring
# carries the exact rule and the error it produces.

# --- Model provider credentials — **not read from this file** (the assistant is
# model-agnostic; you need a key for whichever provider `default_model` names).
# Every other line here is an `ASSISTANT_*` setting, which `Settings` reads out
# of `.env`. A provider key is not a setting of this system at all: it is read
# from the **process environment** by the provider library that makes the call,
# and nothing copies this file into the environment. Export it in the shell that
# starts the **hub** — the only process that talks to a model — and a hub started
# without it refuses to start rather than failing at the first request:
#   export ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=...
#   export OPENAI_API_KEY=...
# Those two are the vendors whose libraries ship in the distribution (ADR-0061).
# Naming any other provider in a model route below is allowed and fails at the
# hub's start, not here, because resolving it needs that vendor's extra
# installed.

# --- Deployment basics.
# The directory the hub owns exclusively: its SQLite stores and its instance
# lock. Must be absolute; unset, a directory under your home is used.
# ASSISTANT_DATA_DIR=/home/you/.ai-assistant
# Root log level.
# ASSISTANT_LOG_LEVEL=INFO
# The model route in pydantic-ai `provider:model` form, and the ordered
# fallbacks it tries when the first fails (comma-separated). Export a key in the
# hub's environment, above, for whichever provider you name here.
# ASSISTANT_DEFAULT_MODEL=anthropic:claude-opus-4-8
# ASSISTANT_FALLBACK_MODELS=openai:gpt-5
# Which embedder backs semantic memory retrieval. `hashing` is deterministic and
# non-semantic — offline runs, tests and CI, never a real user's memory.
# ASSISTANT_EMBEDDER=hashing
# IANA timezone for local-time context.
# ASSISTANT_TIMEZONE=Europe/Rome

# --- Calendar source (ADR-0093 §7a) and the upcoming-event producer
# (ADR-0132 §4). The path configures the source the request-path facet reads;
# the producer is armed by its own interval, which needs the path and is off
# while unset. Nothing reads the source into memory on a schedule (ADR-0294 §1).
# **Configuration is not consent**: the hub reads nothing until the user grants
# the source through a client, and `notify` is a scope that `facet` does not
# back-fill. See `readers/calendar.py`'s module docstring for the `vdirsyncer`
# recipe, the ordering it requires, and the grant commands.
# ASSISTANT_CALENDAR_READER_PATH=/home/you/.calendars/calendar.ics
# ASSISTANT_CALENDAR_UPCOMING_INTERVAL=PT5M
# ASSISTANT_CALENDAR_UPCOMING_LEAD=PT30M

# --- Sending email (ADR-0148 §6). Set both or the `send_email` tool is not
# registered at all. The connection is a reference the provisioner minted — a
# UUID, copied from `assistant connections`, never invented here. The endpoint is
# `smtps://host[:port]` or `smtp+starttls://host[:port]`, and the transport is
# pinned to exactly this text and opens no other.
# ASSISTANT_SEND_EMAIL_CONNECTION=6a1f7c3e-0b2d-4e5a-9c81-0d3f5b7a2e64
# ASSISTANT_SEND_EMAIL_ENDPOINT=smtps://smtp.example.net

# --- Reaching the hub from another device (ADR-0124). Unset, the hub binds only
# its loopback Unix socket and a client on the same machine uses that socket.
# Both addresses are a literal IP on the overlay — never a name, never a
# wildcard, never a public address — and the two ports must match.
# ASSISTANT_HUB_REMOTE_ADDRESS=100.64.0.1      # on the hub
# ASSISTANT_HUB_REMOTE_PORT=50084
# ASSISTANT_REMOTE_HUB_ADDRESS=100.64.0.1      # on each client
# ASSISTANT_REMOTE_HUB_PORT=50084

# --- Serving a browser on another overlay device (ADR-0174 §8, ADR-0202 §8).
# `assistant gateway` always binds a loopback listener on this machine, and that
# address is not configurable. These five add a *second* listener on an overlay
# address, for a browser on another device — a phone. The address is the switch:
# it is the literal overlay address of the machine the gateway runs on, and
# unset there is no second listener. Set it and the other four come with it.
#
# That second listener serves **HTTPS and nothing else**, on a certificate your
# overlay obtains for this machine's own overlay name — `tailscale cert
# laptop.example.ts.net` writes the two files. Both paths are refused at load if
# the address is unset, if either is set without the other, or if the address is
# set without them; the gateway then refuses at start on anything only the
# machine can answer, including a certificate that does not cover every name in
# `..._HOST_NAMES`. The gateway checks owner and mode on both files — no
# permission at all to group or other on the key, no group or other *write* on the
# certificate — which is not a check that nobody else can read the key, since an
# ACL survives an owner-only mode and none is read. `chmod 0600` the key, keep both
# where only you can write, and renew before the certificate expires: a renewed one
# takes effect at the next start.
#
# `..._BROWSER_DEVICES` is the permission — the overlay identities whose browsers
# may exchange a bootstrap value; comma-separated, empty means none, so a
# listener configured on serves its assets and mints no remote session until you
# name a device, and it is refused at load while the address is unset rather than
# ignored. `..._HOST_NAMES` is the authority a `Host` header may name, so you type
# a name on the phone rather than an address; the gateway resolves nothing and
# never uses a name as a destination. It is **not** optional here — every name in
# it must be one the certificate carries, and an empty list is refused at start.
# ASSISTANT_GATEWAY_REMOTE_ADDRESS=100.64.0.2
# ASSISTANT_GATEWAY_REMOTE_BROWSER_DEVICES=nQ8xY2ab3411CNTRL
# ASSISTANT_GATEWAY_REMOTE_HOST_NAMES=laptop.example.ts.net
# ASSISTANT_GATEWAY_REMOTE_TLS_CERTIFICATE=/home/you/.assistant/laptop.example.ts.net.crt
# ASSISTANT_GATEWAY_REMOTE_TLS_KEY=/home/you/.assistant/laptop.example.ts.net.key

# --- Serving a hosted owner through the sign-in edge (ADR-0327). A browser on the
# public network reaches the gateway only through the sign-in edge, which signs
# every request it forwards; these add a *hosted* listener on this box for the
# edge to forward to. The address is the switch: unset, there is no hosted
# listener. Set it and the other six come with it — the listener configured in
# part is refused at load, and so is any of the six set while the address is
# unset.
#
# The address is a literal loopback address on a gateway that shares the box's
# network, or, for a gateway in a container whose hosted port is published to
# the host's loopback and nothing else, the container's own private-use address;
# a wildcard, an overlay or a public address is refused. The listener speaks
# plain HTTP to the edge on the box and binds `gateway_hosted_port`, which has a
# working default and is refused equal to `gateway_port`: point the edge at it,
# and set it here only to move it. How long an assertion may live is a bound
# with its own default too, `gateway_edge_assertion_max_age`.
#
# `..._HOST_NAME` is the door's public hostname exactly as a browser writes it —
# lower case, no scheme, no port, no path. `..._DEVICE` is the one device the
# hosted owner's browsers act as at the hub: a name no other device of that hub
# holds (list them with `ai-assistant-device` first), never `hub`, and not also
# in `..._REMOTE_BROWSER_DEVICES`. It can do nothing until you give it roles with
# `ai-assistant-device` after its first request registers it.
#
# The edge's settings are what you copy from the edge at setup. `..._PUBLIC_KEYS`
# are its Ed25519 **public** keys, comma-separated, each the raw 32 bytes in
# unpadded base64url (RFC 8037's `x`); the edge's private key never comes here.
# To rotate, add the new key, restart, and remove the old one once the edge no
# longer signs with it. Issuer, audience and subject are compared literally with
# what each assertion carries; the audience names this hub, and the subject is
# the edge's opaque id for the one account this door admits — copied from the
# edge's own account record, never an email address, never a list. The key below
# was generated for this file and its private half discarded, so no edge signs
# with it: a door configured with it admits nothing.
# ASSISTANT_GATEWAY_HOSTED_ADDRESS=127.0.0.1
# ASSISTANT_GATEWAY_HOSTED_HOST_NAME=door.example.com
# ASSISTANT_GATEWAY_HOSTED_DEVICE=web-owner
# ASSISTANT_GATEWAY_EDGE_PUBLIC_KEYS=3ICW70EyD2H7-dWf-Kk2W38WYvDG_zweF0q3bMyVfTc
# ASSISTANT_GATEWAY_EDGE_ISSUER=https://edge.example.com
# ASSISTANT_GATEWAY_EDGE_AUDIENCE=hub-owner.example.com
# ASSISTANT_GATEWAY_EDGE_SUBJECT=acct-7f3c9a2e5b104d68

# --- The Telegram line (ADR-0323, ADR-0330). The connection and the account are
# the switch: set both or neither. The connection is the reference
# `assistant connect` printed for the line's credential, and the account is the
# user's own Telegram id, whose private chat with the bot is the one chat the line
# writes to. Its poll wait and bounds have working defaults.
#
# Relayed mode, for a hosted person reached through the relay on the box: set the
# relay's origin, and the line reaches the relay with the hub's relay credential
# (connected as a connection of its own, never one that held a bot token) instead
# of Telegram with a bot token. The origin names a host, never an IP address, and
# no path. The certificates are the relay's CA certificate, as `relay cert show`
# prints it with its line breaks removed, comma-separated during a rotation. The
# hub trusts the relay by these alone, never by the system's authorities. Unset
# the origin and the line is in direct mode. The certificate below was generated
# for this file and its private half discarded, so no relay presents it.
# `docs/guide/telegram-relay.md` walks through the setup.
# ASSISTANT_TELEGRAM_CONNECTION=conn-0001
# ASSISTANT_TELEGRAM_USER_ID=424242
# ASSISTANT_TELEGRAM_RELAY_ORIGIN=https://telegram-relay:8443
# ASSISTANT_TELEGRAM_RELAY_CERTIFICATES=-----BEGIN CERTIFICATE----------END CERTIFICATE-----

# --- Permission thresholds: what an action needs confirming for, and what is
# refused outright. Risk is `low|medium|high|critical`, reversibility is
# `reversible|recoverable|irreversible`, and `none` disables that test. Unset,
# nothing is refused outright.
# ASSISTANT_CONFIRM_AT_RISK=medium
# ASSISTANT_CONFIRM_AT_REVERSIBILITY=irreversible
# ASSISTANT_DENY_AT_RISK=critical
# ASSISTANT_DENY_AT_REVERSIBILITY=none

# --- What the world may cost (ADR-0194 §1, §2). **The currency comes first**: a
# ceiling or an allowance set without it is refused at load, and the currency set
# alone arms reporting only — totals are computed and readable and nothing is ever
# refused. The two ceilings bind independently, and a day ceiling above the month
# ceiling is accepted; it is simply never the binding one.
# The currency the ceilings and their totals are denominated in, ISO-4217.
# ASSISTANT_WORLD_SPEND_CURRENCY=USD
# Most the world may cost across a calendar month, and across a calendar day, in
# the configured zone. Each is unbounded while unset.
# ASSISTANT_WORLD_SPEND_MONTH_CEILING=20.00
# ASSISTANT_WORLD_SPEND_DAY_CEILING=2.00
# What one call of UNKNOWN price is accounted at; strictly greater than zero.
# Unset, such a call is refused rather than counted as costing nothing.
# ASSISTANT_WORLD_SPEND_UNKNOWN_ALLOWANCE=0.01
